                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Looking through the cheap games available on GreenManGaming I stumbled upon this 'Tcheco in the Castle of Lucio' and I decided to check it out based on the thumbnail only to be prompt with an NES like platformer and boy oh boy for 79 cents I decided to give it a shot.\n\nYou play as Tcheco the Adventure Boy as he stumbles his way through a mysterious castle of Lucio, he will come across wild dogs spewing fire, paintings that shoot lasers, the Brazilian favorite soccer (futbol sorry), and many other deadly obstacles. Will he make his way out?\n\nEasy to learn and hard to master, this game is one of those titles where you play over and over trying to get to the end of the 65 stages and it's one of those games that's addicting because you wanna see how far you can get. \n\nThe gameplay itself is pretty solid and feels very responsive and even though some of the obstacles you encounter can be cheap it at least feels like it builds up and you have an actual chance especially if you play several times to learn patterns or layouts. You only really jump, jump your way to a key or two to open a path to the next room facing whatever obstacles may be ahead and Tcheco keeps throwing new things at you so it never felt repetitive or bad. There are boss fights present, but it's only really you jumping on buttons to shoot rockets, so  you don't really attack anything aside from this. It overall does feel like a solid NES game.\n\nAudio is great and on par with what an NES game would/should sound like. Music is also fantastic. I'm not an audiophile so forgive my lack of in depth talk here.\n\nKind of a long review for a short little game, but I liked it more than I thought I was going to. If anything above sounds good to you then pick it up as it's only a dollar or even go to somewhere like GMG and pick it up for a little less. I will be picking up the sequel 'Skatemasta Tcheco' as well.",
